# The brick grid, one frame per row of it.
#
# Four frames, and the frame index **is** the grid row index counting down from
# `game::BRICK_TOP` — `art::brick_frame` is the one place that mapping is
# written, and `the_brick_rows_draw_the_frames_they_are_meant_to` asserts each
# row of a live grid cuts its own cell of the strip. A single brick tinted four
# ways would have been the other answer; it is not what a sprite sheet is for,
# and it cannot give the four rows different shading, which is what makes the
# grid read as four courses of masonry rather than one wall recoloured.
#
# 24 x 8 texels at `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT` = 10 is exactly
# `BRICK_WIDTH` x `BRICK_HEIGHT` = 2.4 x 0.8 world units, so the picture is the
# collider, at one texel per sprite unit.
#
# Each frame is the same bevel: a dark rim, a lit top and left, a shaded bottom
# and right. Every brick in a row is the same picture, so the shading is what
# stops ten identical rectangles reading as one long bar.
#
# **Bricks take one hit.** `game::handle_brick_hit` despawns on contact and
# there is no hit counter to draw, so there is no cracked second frame here. If
# a brick ever gains a second life, this is where its damaged frame goes.
crpix: 24 8 4 13 1

